# README.ai — VerdictPay (hackathon branch)

This file is a working guide for coding agents. Prefer the source code and `package.json` as the source of truth when they disagree with prose docs. The original stock-research app (The Verdict Chamber) lives on `main`; do not change `main` unless explicitly asked.

## Project at a glance

VerdictPay is a Next.js 15 / React 19 / TypeScript app. A user describes a money decision (purchase, subscription or invoice). The server computes deterministic evidence, a sequential five-agent jury (Advocate, Skeptic, Budget Officer, Analyst, Judge) streams its deliberation over server-sent events, and the judge returns a structured PROCEED / MODIFY / REJECT verdict. An approved verdict is paid through the PayPal sandbox (Orders v2 + JS SDK) for exactly the approved amount, after explicit user confirmation.

## Setup and commands

- Node.js 22.12 or newer; npm with `package-lock.json`.
- Install: `npm ci` · Develop: `npm run dev` · Build: `npm run build` · Serve: `npm start`
- Checks: `npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm test` (Vitest)
- Copy `.env.local.example` to `.env.local`. Keys: `GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY` (or `GEMINI_API_KEY`), optional `GEMINI_MODEL` (default `gemini-3.8-flash`), `PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID`, `PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET`, `VERDICT_SIGNING_SECRET`. All optional: without Gemini a labelled demo jury runs; without PayPal the payment step records a labelled simulated payment.

## Routes

- `/`, `/verdict/new`, `/verdict/[id]` (live review; redirects to the saved review if one exists), `/verdict/history`, `/verdict/history/[id]`
- `POST /api/jury` — validates `{ decision }`, recomputes evidence, streams `JuryEvent`s; the `verdict` event carries `approvalToken`
- `POST /api/paypal/orders` — `{ approvalToken }` → `{ id }`
- `POST /api/paypal/orders/[id]/capture` → `{ orderId, status, captureId, amount }`

## Architecture map

- `lib/validation/decision.ts` — the decision schema shared by form, API and storage; types are `z.infer`ed from it.
- `lib/decision/evidence.ts` — deterministic evidence (budget share, first-year cost, overlap, urgency, red flags). Pure; runs on client and server.
- `lib/agents/prompts.ts` — juror system prompts and evidence serialisation.
- `lib/agents/orchestrate.ts` — sequential jury, Gemini calls (`streamText` for counsel, `generateObject` for the judge), demo fallback, event emission including a per-agent `simulated` flag.
- `lib/agents/verdict.ts` — judge schema, `normalizeVerdict` (money rules), `simulateVerdict` (demo judge).
- `lib/payments/approval.ts` — HMAC-signed, expiring approvals.
- `lib/payments/paypal.ts` — Orders v2 client (sandbox host only), cached OAuth token, `PayPal-Request-Id` idempotency.
- `lib/utils/storage.ts` — localStorage persistence under `vp.*`, validated on read.
- `components/verdict/` — `DecisionForm`, `EvidencePanel`, `JuryRoom`, `AgentCard`, `JurorRail`, `VerdictCard`, `PaymentPanel`.

## Invariants to preserve

- Money rules live in code, never only in prompts: PROCEED pays the requested amount, MODIFY never exceeds it, REJECT pays 0 (`normalizeVerdict`). Keep the tests in `lib/agents/verdict.test.ts` passing.
- A PayPal order may only be created from a valid `verifyApproval` token, for the amount inside the token. Never accept an amount from the client.
- `PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET`, `VERDICT_SIGNING_SECRET` and the Gemini key stay server-side. Only `PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID` is exposed (as `NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID` via `next.config.mjs`).
- The jury is sequential; each agent sees evidence plus prior arguments. If you change event names or payloads, update `lib/types/verdict.ts`, the API route and `JuryRoom` together.
- `runJury` must always emit exactly one `verdict` event unless the client disconnects; demo output must stay labelled (`simulated`).
- Evidence numbers come from `buildEvidence`; prompts present them and must not invent prices, plans or merchant facts. User-typed fields are untrusted data in prompts.
- Keep the "AI decision support, not financial advice" wording and the explicit confirmation before checkout.

## Working conventions for agents

- Inspect the route, component and shared type before changing them; follow existing patterns and keep changes localized.
- Validate at API boundaries with Zod; treat request bodies and stored records as untrusted.
- Do not print `.env.local` contents. Use sandbox credentials only.
- Prefer existing dependencies; do not add a package for a small helper.
- Do not claim a command passed unless it was run.

## Deployment notes

`vercel.json` selects the Mumbai (`bom1`) region. `app/api/jury/route.ts` caps duration at 60 seconds; five sequential model calls may need more on slow models. The PayPal host is hard-wired to the sandbox in `lib/payments/paypal.ts`.
